---
import { getImage } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CtaBar from '../components/CtaBar.astro';
import WorkCard from '../components/WorkCard.astro';
import { pricing } from '../data/pricing';
import { findWork } from '../data/work';
import FrameMaterials from '../components/diagrams/FrameMaterials.astro';
import UFactorGauge from '../components/diagrams/UFactorGauge.astro';
import featuresHeroSrc from '../assets/hero/window-features.png';

const ogPhoto = findWork('stone-craftsman')!;

// Full-bleed hero background. AI-generated macro detail shot, not a real
// Clearview unit, so it is captioned as such.
const heroBg = await getImage({ src: featuresHeroSrc, width: 2400, format: 'webp', quality: 76 });

const materials = pricing.materials;
const brands = pricing.brands;
const styles = pricing.openings.filter((opening) => !opening.isDoor);
const doors = pricing.openings.filter((opening) => opening.isDoor);

const stylePhotos = [
  { id: 'blue-slider', label: 'Slider' },
  { id: 'new-unit-interior', label: 'Double-hung' },
  { id: 'blue-hung', label: 'Single-hung' },
  { id: 'green-craftsman-upper', label: 'Picture' },
]
  .map(({ id, label }) => ({ label, photo: findWork(id) }))
  .filter((entry): entry is { label: string; photo: NonNullable<ReturnType<typeof findWork>> } => Boolean(entry.photo));
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Window features"
  description="What's actually in a Clearview window: vinyl or fiberglass, Cascade or Milgard, the glass package, and every style we install in Vancouver, WA and Clark County."
  path="/window-features"
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Window features', href: '/window-features' }]}
  ogImageSrc={ogPhoto.image}
  ogImageAlt={ogPhoto.alt}
>
  <section class="page-hero page-hero-photo" style={`background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--black) 58%, transparent), color-mix(in srgb, var(--black) 88%, transparent)), url(${heroBg.src})`}>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Window features</p>
      <h1>The window, not just the price.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A quote is a number. This page is what that number is actually for — the material, the
        glass, the brand Mark orders it from, and the shape it comes in.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="hero-photo-credit">AI-generated imagery — not an actual Clearview unit</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap is-narrow" data-reveal>
      <p class="rule-label">Why it matters</p>
      <h2>A small part of the wall. A large part of the bill.</h2>
      <p>
        Windows are a small share of a house's square footage and a large share of what it loses
        in a Washington winter. A failed seal, a tired weatherstrip, and a single pane of glass
        all show up the same way — a draft at the lock, a cold chair by the window, a heating
        bill that climbs a little more each year the unit gets older.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of that means every window needs replacing. It means the choices below are worth
        understanding before you pay for one — whether that's with us or anyone else.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-sunk">
    <div class="wrap" data-reveal>
      <p class="rule-label">What it's made of</p>
      <h2>Vinyl or fiberglass.</h2>
      <div class="compare">
        {materials.map((material) => (
          <article class="compare-card">
            <h3>{material.label}</h3>
            <p class="compare-blurb">{material.blurb}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="diagram-slot">
        <FrameMaterials />
      </div>
      <p class="fine-print">
        We install both. Which one makes sense depends on the opening size and what you want
        out of it — vinyl for most standard openings, fiberglass where a wide slider or picture
        window would otherwise flex. Mark will say which on site, not from a photo.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap" data-reveal>
      <p class="rule-label">Which window</p>
      <h2>Cascade or Milgard.</h2>
      <div class="compare">
        {brands.map((brand) => (
          <article class="compare-card">
            <h3>{brand.label}</h3>
            <p class="compare-blurb">{brand.blurb}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="fine-print">
        Same crew, same install, a different window in the opening. Ask which one a quote is
        written around, and why — Mark will tell you what he is ordering and what it costs before
        anything goes on paper.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-sunk">
    <div class="wrap is-narrow" data-reveal>
      <p class="rule-label">Glass</p>
      <h2>Double- or triple-pane, Low-E either way.</h2>
      <p>
        Every unit we install is an insulated glass package — double- or triple-pane, with a
        Low-E coating tuned for a Pacific Northwest heating season rather than the hot, dry
        climate that coating was originally built for. That is the difference between glass that
        looks clear and glass that is actually doing the job here.
      </p>
      <p>
        A cloudy or fogged pane is a different problem entirely — a failed seal, not a coating
        choice. <a href="/guides/fogged-windows">How to tell the difference</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="diagram-slot">
        <UFactorGauge />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="styles">
    <div class="wrap is-wide" data-reveal>
      <p class="rule-label">Shapes and operation</p>
      <h2>Every opening, however it moves.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        The style does not change what is in the glass — it changes how the sash opens, and how
        much of the frame you see once it is shut.
      </p>

      <div class="style-list">
        {styles.map((style) => (
          <article class="style-row">
            <h3>{style.label}</h3>
            <p>{style.blurb}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>

      {stylePhotos.length > 0 && (
        <div class="work-grid style-photos">
          {stylePhotos.map(({ label, photo }) => (
            <WorkCard image={photo.image} alt={photo.alt} caption={photo.caption} kind={label} />
          ))}
        </div>
      )}

      <p class="fine-print">
        Arches, transoms, and other non-rectangular openings are measured and priced
        individually — not on this list, but not unusual either.
        {doors.length > 0 && (
          <>
            {' '}Doors — {doors.map((door) => door.label.toLowerCase()).join(' and ')} — are not
            technically windows, but Mark sets both on the same trip if you are doing a whole
            elevation. More on <a href="/sliding-glass-doors">sliding glass doors</a>.
          </>
        )}
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-sunk">
    <div class="wrap is-narrow" data-reveal>
      <p class="rule-label">Hardware and color</p>
      <h2>Whatever the line actually offers.</h2>
      <p>
        Locking hardware, interior and exterior color, and grid patterns all come from whichever
        line Mark orders — Cascade's options are not identical to Milgard's. Rather than publish a
        swatch board that might not match what ships, he brings the real samples to the measure,
        so you are choosing from what is actually available.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap is-narrow" data-reveal>
      <p class="rule-label">Warranty</p>
      <h2>Ask what's covered, and by whom.</h2>
      <p>
        The glass and hardware carry the manufacturer's own warranty — Cascade and Milgard each
        write theirs differently. The install is a separate promise, from Mark. Ask him to spell
        out both before you sign: what's covered, for how long, and who you call if something
        needs it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBar
    heading="See it in your own opening."
    body="The right material, brand, and style depend on the house. Mark measures, then tells you exactly what he's ordering and why."
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Full-bleed photo hero. The image is AI-generated macro detail, not a
     photo of a real Clearview unit — hence the small credit line. */
  .page-hero-photo {
    position: relative;
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
  }

  .hero-photo-credit {
    position: absolute;
    right: var(--space-s);
    bottom: var(--space-2xs);
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--step--2);
    color: var(--on-dark-faint);
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  /* Two comparisons on this page — material and brand — share this device: a
     pair of cards rather than prose, because the choice is genuinely binary
     and a reader scanning for "which one" should not have to parse a
     paragraph to find it. */
  .compare {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    margin: var(--space-l) 0 var(--space-m);
  }

  .diagram-slot {
    margin: var(--space-l) 0;
  }

  .compare-card {
    padding: var(--space-m);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--paper-raised);
  }

  .compare-card h3 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2xs);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
  }

  .compare-blurb {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  /* The style list is meant to be scanned in order, like the six-signs band
     on the homepage — a spine down the middle of the page, not a card grid
     implying the eight are interchangeable. */
  .style-list {
    margin: var(--space-l) 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .style-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 10rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-m);
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: var(--space-s) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .style-row h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--step-0);
  }

  .style-row p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .style-photos {
    margin-top: var(--space-l);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .compare {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .style-row {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-3xs);
    }

    .style-photos {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
